Skewed Large Language Models as Planning and Execution Risks: Information Distortion, Reliance, and Cascading Error in AI-Assisted Decision Systems

The central concern is not merely that an LLM can produce a bad answer, but that a distorted model can become part of the causal chain that produces a distorted plan and subsequently a flawed real-world action. This paper frames “skewing” broadly—through biased inputs, selective evidence, adversarial prompting, contaminated context, fine-tuning, retrieval data, or conversational steering—and distinguish deliberate manipulation from ordinary model error. 

Abstract

Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used not merely to generate text but to assist with analysis, planning, prioritization, decision support, tool selection, workflow construction, and execution of multi-step tasks. This expanded role creates a distinctive class of risk: an LLM may first be systematically or situationally skewed, and a human or software system may subsequently rely upon the resulting outputs when constructing or executing a plan. The resulting failure is not necessarily attributable to a single false statement. Rather, a distortion introduced at one stage can propagate through interpretation, planning, delegation, tool use, execution, and subsequent evaluation.

This paper develops a conceptual framework for this phenomenon. It distinguishes ordinary model error from _skew_, where the information or interpretive conditions presented to a model systematically shift its outputs in a particular direction. Skew may arise through biased or incomplete source material, selective context, leading prompts, adversarial inputs, contaminated retrieval corpora, conversational pressure, model-specific behavioral tendencies, or deliberate manipulation. Recent research documents sycophancy—the tendency of language models to conform to user-stated beliefs—and demonstrates that such behavior can persist across multi-turn interactions.

The paper argues that reliance on a skewed model can create a **cascading epistemic-to-operational failure**. A distorted representation of the situation may produce a distorted recommendation; the recommendation may become a plan; the plan may determine which information is sought and which alternatives are excluded; execution may then create real-world conditions that appear to confirm the original model output. The result can be a feedback loop in which an initially weak distortion becomes increasingly consequential as it is translated into action.

This risk is closely related to the established literature on automation bias and over-reliance on decision-support systems. Systematic reviews have found that users can accept erroneous automated recommendations, particularly under conditions of cognitive load, complexity, and time pressure.  PubMed Central (PMC)+1  LLMs introduce additional complications because their outputs are linguistically persuasive, context-sensitive, adaptive, and capable of generating coherent rationales even when their underlying conclusions are incorrect. NIST consequently identifies “confabulation”—confidently presented erroneous or false generated content—as a significant generative-AI risk, particularly where users may act upon the resulting information in consequential settings.

The paper proposes a layered model encompassing **input skew, representational skew, inferential skew, planning skew, execution skew, and feedback skew**. It further argues that safeguards must address the entire chain rather than merely model accuracy. Independent verification, competing hypotheses, source provenance, explicit uncertainty, plan-level validation, human accountability, execution checkpoints, and post-action review can reduce the probability that a distorted model becomes an unexamined operational authority.

The central thesis is that the security and reliability problem of skewed LLMs is fundamentally a **systems problem of dependency propagation**: once an AI system becomes an intermediate reasoning component upon which later decisions depend, errors or distortions in that component can acquire consequences far beyond the original output.

