 # 1\. Introduction

 Large language models have moved from relatively passive text-generation systems toward increasingly active forms of decision support.

 They summarize information.

 They compare alternatives.

 They identify considerations.

 They formulate strategies.

 They construct plans.

 They generate task sequences.

 They select or recommend tools.

 They interpret information returned by tools.

 They revise plans in response to changing conditions.

 And, in agentic systems, they may participate directly in execution.

 This creates a fundamentally different risk profile from that associated with a model that merely produces an isolated paragraph.

 If an isolated answer is wrong, the error may end with the answer.

 If an LLM is embedded in a planning or execution chain, the error may instead become an **input to subsequent reasoning**.

 The relevant chain can therefore be represented as:

 $$
\text{information}
\rightarrow
\text{model interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{assessment}
\rightarrow
\text{plan}
\rightarrow
\text{execution}
\rightarrow
\text{new information}.
$$

 An error introduced near the beginning may therefore influence every subsequent stage.

 The problem becomes particularly interesting when the model is not simply inaccurate but **skewed**.

 A model can be skewed when its outputs are systematically shifted by the information, framing, context, incentives, or interaction conditions surrounding it.

 The distinction matters.

 A random error might cause one incorrect conclusion.

 A systematic skew can cause a _family_ of related conclusions to move in the same direction.

 That difference has major implications for planning.

 A planner operating with a systematically distorted model may not merely make one mistake. It may construct an internally coherent plan whose assumptions are wrong throughout.

 Recent research demonstrates that LLMs can exhibit sycophancy, meaning that they may shift their responses toward a user's stated beliefs or positions. Studies have found such behavior across multiple models and in multi-turn settings, suggesting that conversational context can itself influence model judgments.

 The question examined here is therefore:

 > **What happens when an LLM is systematically skewed and then becomes a relied-upon component of planning or execution?**

 The answer requires considering not merely model accuracy, but **information integrity, reliance, dependency, verification, feedback, and organizational responsibility**.

