 # 4\. From Skewed Information to Skewed Representation

 The first major stage of cascading failure is representational.

 A planning system cannot reason directly over reality.

 It reasons over a representation of reality.

 Thus:

 $$
\text{world}
\rightarrow
\text{information}
\rightarrow
\text{model representation}.
$$

 If the representation is distorted, subsequent reasoning inherits the distortion.

 This produces an important principle:

 > **A planning system can reason correctly over an incorrect representation and still produce an incorrect plan.**

 The quality of reasoning and the quality of premises are therefore distinct dimensions.

 A sophisticated reasoning model cannot necessarily compensate for information that has systematically excluded the relevant alternatives.

---

 # 5\. From Representation to Planning

 Planning introduces another transformation:

 $$
\text{representation}
\rightarrow
\text{objective}
\rightarrow
\text{constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{options}
\rightarrow
\text{plan}.
$$

 Each stage can amplify an earlier distortion.

 Suppose the model incorrectly believes that:

 - condition A is highly probable;
- condition B is unlikely;
- option X is feasible;
- constraint Y does not apply.

 It may construct a plan optimized for that representation.

 The plan may be internally coherent.

 Its failure may therefore not be visible through superficial examination.

 Research specifically examining erroneous planning in LLM agents finds that LLM-based agents can produce erroneous plans for complex tasks and proposes evaluating plans against constraints derived from user requirements.

 This suggests that plan validation must examine whether the plan satisfies the _actual constraints_, not merely whether the plan sounds reasonable.

---

 # 6\. Planning Can Amplify Small Distortions

 An important property of planning is that consequences can be nonlinear.

 A small change in an assumption can produce a large change in a plan.

 Consider:

 $$
A \rightarrow B \rightarrow C.
$$

 If a small change in the probability of A causes the planner to choose a completely different branch, then a small epistemic distortion can produce a large operational divergence.

 This can be represented as:

 $$
\Delta \text{belief} \ll \Delta \text{plan}
$$

 in situations involving thresholds or branching decisions.

 Examples include:

 - whether an action is necessary;
- whether a contingency is activated;
- whether a resource is allocated;
- whether a risk is considered acceptable;
- whether an alternative is investigated.

 Thus, planning systems can function as **error amplifiers**.

---

 # 7\. From Plan to Execution

 The most consequential transition occurs when the plan is acted upon.

 Before execution, the error is primarily epistemic.

 After execution, it can become operational.

 The chain becomes:

 $$
\text{skewed model}
\rightarrow
\text{skewed assessment}
\rightarrow
\text{skewed plan}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{real-world consequence}.
$$

 This distinction is critical.

 A mistaken answer in a conversation may be harmless.

 A mistaken answer incorporated into an operational plan may not be.

 The same underlying model error can therefore have radically different consequences depending upon where it sits within a decision architecture.

---

 # 8\. Execution Assistance Creates a Distinct Risk

 The risk increases further when an LLM assists with execution rather than merely planning.

 An execution-oriented system may:

 - generate commands;
- populate forms;
- invoke tools;
- select actions;
- sequence operations;
- modify documents;
- communicate instructions;
- or continuously revise a plan.

 At this stage, the model becomes an intermediate control component.

 Its outputs are no longer merely informational.

 They can become **causal inputs to external systems**.

 That creates a distinction between:

 $$
\text{AI as advisor}
$$

 and

 $$
\text{AI as action-producing component}.
$$

 The latter requires substantially stronger safeguards because an erroneous recommendation can become an erroneous action without an additional human interpretation step.

---

 # 9\. Automation Bias and the Human Reliance Problem

 The human side of this problem has substantial precedent.

 Automation-bias research finds that users can over-rely on decision-support systems and accept incorrect automated recommendations. A systematic review identified both errors of commission—following incorrect advice—and errors of omission—failing to act because an automated system did not prompt the action.

 A later systematic review found automation bias in single-task settings as well as multitasking environments, with verification complexity and cognitive load being important factors.

 The relevance to LLMs is straightforward but not identical.

 LLMs differ from traditional decision-support systems because they communicate in natural language.

 They can therefore produce:

  - explanations;
- rationales;
- caveats;
- narratives;
- structured plans;
- confidence-like language;
- and apparent responsiveness.

 This can make an output psychologically persuasive even when its underlying factual basis is weak.

 NIST specifically warns that users may act upon confident but erroneous generative-AI outputs and that fabricated reasoning can further mislead users.

---

 # 10\. The Persuasion–Reliance Interaction

 The danger is therefore not simply:

 $$
\text{bad output}.
$$

 It is:

 $$
\text{bad output}
+
\text{persuasive presentation}
+
\text{high reliance}
=
\text{elevated downstream risk}.
$$

 This does not mean that LLMs necessarily manipulate users.

 Rather, natural-language fluency changes the human-computer interface.

 A system can be wrong while sounding competent.

 That creates a calibration problem.

 Users must distinguish:

 > “The system produced a plausible explanation”

 from:

 > “The system has established that the explanation is correct.”

 Those are fundamentally different claims.

---

 # 11\. Skew Plus Reliance

 The central theoretical relationship can be represented as:

 $$
R = S \times D \times A
$$

 where:

 - $S$ = degree of systematic skew;
- $D$ = dependency on the model;
- $A$ = actionability of the output.

 Again, this is a conceptual model rather than a validated quantitative equation.

 The idea is that a mildly skewed model may present little risk when used as one input among many.

 The same skew can become consequential when:

 - the model is treated as authoritative;
- alternatives are not independently considered;
- the output determines the plan;
- and the plan directly determines action.

---

 # 12\. The Dependency Chain

 A particularly important concept is **epistemic dependency**.

 Suppose:

 $$
X \rightarrow M \rightarrow P \rightarrow E
$$

 where:

 - $X$ = information;
- $M$ = model interpretation;
- $P$ = plan;
- $E$ = execution.

 If $P$ depends heavily upon $M$, then errors in $M$ propagate into $P$.

 If $E$ depends heavily upon $P$, the propagation continues.

 The resulting risk is not simply the probability that the model is wrong.

 It is:

 $$
P(\text{harm}) =
P(M\text{ wrong})
\times
P(P\text{ depends on M})
\times
P(E\text{ follows P})
\times
P(\text{consequential failure}).
$$

 This decomposition illustrates why **dependency architecture** matters as much as raw model accuracy.

---

 # 13\. Correlated Error

 An especially serious problem arises when multiple planning stages depend upon the same skewed source.

 Suppose an LLM:

 1. interprets the situation;
2. identifies relevant risks;
3. proposes options;
4. selects an option;
5. evaluates its own plan.

 If every stage uses the same model and context, the apparent redundancy may be illusory.

 The model is effectively checking itself.

 This can produce:

 $$
\text{shared assumption}
\rightarrow
\text{shared conclusion}
\rightarrow
\text{apparent confirmation}.
$$

 Independent agreement is much stronger evidence than repeated agreement generated from the same underlying assumptions.

---

 # 14\. Self-Confirmation Loops

 A particularly dangerous architecture is:

 $$
M \rightarrow P \rightarrow E \rightarrow X'
$$

 where $X'$ is new information generated by executing the model's own plan.

 The new information may then be fed back into the model:

 $$
X' \rightarrow M'.
$$

 This creates:

 $$
M \rightarrow P \rightarrow E \rightarrow X' \rightarrow M'.
$$

 If the original model was skewed, the system may begin interpreting the consequences of its own actions through the same skew.

 This is a **self-confirming feedback loop**.

 The system can therefore become increasingly confident without becoming increasingly correct.

---

 # 15\. Action-Induced Evidence

 Execution creates another complication: actions change the world.

 If an LLM predicts that a certain condition will occur and a human acts in accordance with that prediction, the resulting state may partly reflect the action itself.

 Consequently:

 $$
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{changed environment}.
$$

 Later observations cannot always be treated as independent confirmation of the original prediction.

 The system has partially generated its own evidence.

 This is particularly important for planning systems because planning is not passive observation.

 Planning is intervention.

---

 # 16\. Confirmation Through Selective Observation

 Skew can also affect what information is subsequently collected.

 Suppose an LLM produces hypothesis H.

 A human then asks the system:

 > “What evidence would support H?”

 rather than:

 > “What evidence would distinguish H from competing hypotheses?”

 The information-gathering process itself becomes skewed.

 This can produce:

 $$
\text{skewed hypothesis}
\rightarrow
\text{skewed information search}
\rightarrow
\text{apparently supporting evidence}.
$$

 The resulting confidence can increase even though the underlying hypothesis was never independently tested.

---

 # 17\. The Difference Between Assistance and Delegation

 An important governance distinction is whether the LLM is:

 ### Assisting

 The human remains responsible for independently determining what should be done.

 or:

 ### Delegated

 The human effectively transfers the reasoning task to the model and accepts its output unless something obviously appears wrong.

 The two may look superficially identical.

 Both involve a prompt and an answer.

 But their epistemic structures differ dramatically.

 In the first:

 $$
\text{human reasoning} + \text{AI input}.
$$

 In the second:

 $$
\text{AI reasoning} \rightarrow \text{human acceptance}.
$$

 The second architecture increases the consequences of model skew.

---

 # 18\. The Problem of Verification

 Verification is frequently proposed as the solution.

 But verification itself has a cost.

 A human who independently reconstructs every model conclusion may effectively perform the task twice.

 Under time pressure or high cognitive load, users may therefore be tempted to perform superficial verification.

 Automation-bias research identifies workload, complexity, and time pressure as factors that can increase reliance on automated decision support.  PubMed Central (PMC)

 Consequently:

 > **A system cannot rely upon verification merely existing in principle. It must make meaningful verification feasible.**

---

 # 19\. Verification Must Be Independent

 There is an important difference between:

 > “Check the model's answer.”

 and:

 > “Independently determine whether the model's answer is supported.”

 The first can become circular.

 For example:

 > Model: “The plan is safe because A, B, and C.”

 > User: “Is the plan safe?”

 > Model: “Yes, because A, B, and C.”

 Nothing independent has occurred.

 A stronger process would involve:

 - separate evidence;
- alternative reasoning;
- independent calculations;
- competing hypotheses;
- domain-specific validation;
- or a second system with meaningfully different information or methodology.

---

 # 20\. Competing Models and Epistemic Diversity

 One possible safeguard is deliberate epistemic diversity.

 Instead of asking one model:

 > “What should we do?”

 a system might separately ask:

 - What assumptions are being made?
- What could falsify them?
- What alternative explanations exist?
- What information is missing?
- What would a skeptical analyst conclude?
- What would change the recommendation?

 The purpose is not to manufacture disagreement.

 It is to reduce the probability that one distorted framing becomes the only representation of the problem.

---

 # 21\. Adversarial Testing of Plans

 A plan should ideally be tested against the conditions that would make it fail.

 This can be conceptualized as:

 $$
\text{plan}
\rightarrow
\text{failure-mode generation}
\rightarrow
\text{stress test}.
$$

 The evaluator should ask:

 - What assumptions does this plan require?
- Which assumptions are uncertain?
- What happens if they are wrong?
- Which step has the largest dependency?
- What information would invalidate the plan?
- What alternative plan would work under different assumptions?

 This transforms validation from “does the plan sound reasonable?” into “under what conditions does the plan fail?”

---

 # 22\. Plan Validity Versus Plan Plausibility

 LLMs are particularly good at producing plausible sequences of language.

 But:

 $$
\text{plausibility} \neq \text{validity}.
$$

 A plausible plan may contain:

 - impossible dependencies;
- missing prerequisites;
- inconsistent constraints;
- unavailable resources;
- incorrect assumptions;
- or steps that do not actually produce their claimed outcomes.

 Research on erroneous planning in LLM agents highlights precisely this issue by defining planning errors in relation to constraint violations rather than linguistic implausibility.

 Thus, plan evaluation should be based on **external constraints**, not merely linguistic coherence.

---

 # 23\. Execution as a Separate Validation Stage

 A plan should not automatically be treated as validated merely because it has been generated.

 A robust architecture can separate:

 $$
\text{planning}
\rightarrow
\text{validation}
\rightarrow
\text{authorization}
\rightarrow
\text{execution}.
$$

 Each stage can have different evidence requirements.

 Planning asks:

 > “What could we do?”

 Validation asks:

 > “Would this actually work under the relevant constraints?”

 Authorization asks:

 > “Should this be done?”

 Execution asks:

 > “Are the conditions still consistent with the plan?”

 This separation reduces the possibility that the same erroneous assumption propagates through every stage.

---

 # 24\. Dynamic Execution Requires Continuous Reassessment

 Plans operate in changing environments.

 Therefore:

 $$
P(t_0) \neq P(t_1)
$$

 may be appropriate even when the original plan was sound.

 A model that becomes anchored to its initial plan may interpret new evidence as noise rather than evidence requiring revision.

 This is another reason that execution assistance should include explicit reassessment points.

 The question should not merely be:

 > “Are we following the plan?”

 but:

 > “Does the evidence still support the assumptions on which the plan was based?”

---

 # 25\. The Security Dimension

 The security significance of model skew depends heavily on where the model sits within an operational architecture.

 A model used to summarize public information has one risk profile.

 A model used to determine access decisions has another.

 A model used to prioritize incident response has another.

 A model used to control external systems has another.

 The same underlying skew can therefore have very different consequences.

 NIST's Generative AI Profile emphasizes that confabulation risks become particularly significant when systems are integrated into consequential decision-making, because users may act upon erroneous outputs.

 The general principle is:

 $$
\text{risk} \propto
\text{model influence}
\times
\text{decision consequence}
\times
\text{difficulty of independent verification}.
$$

---

