 # 27\. The Attacker Does Not Necessarily Need to Control the Model

 A significant theoretical point follows.

 If a person wants to influence an AI-assisted planning system, complete control over the model may be unnecessary.

 Influence can occur at several layers:

 $$
\text{data}
\rightarrow
\text{retrieval}
\rightarrow
\text{prompt}
\rightarrow
\text{context}
\rightarrow
\text{model}
\rightarrow
\text{human}
\rightarrow
\text{execution}.
$$

 Manipulating an upstream input may be sufficient to alter downstream decisions.

 This is analogous to ordinary information operations: changing the information environment can change decisions without directly controlling the decision-maker.

---

 # 28\. The Model as an Epistemic Bottleneck

 An LLM becomes particularly consequential when many downstream decisions depend on a single interpretation.

 For example:

 $$
X \rightarrow LLM \rightarrow
\begin{cases}
P_1\\
P_2\\
P_3\\
P_4
\end{cases}
$$

 If the LLM's interpretation is skewed, all four downstream decisions may inherit the same distortion.

 The model has become an **epistemic bottleneck**.

 This is analogous to correlated failure in engineering: multiple downstream components may appear independent while sharing a single upstream dependency.

---

 # 29\. Organizational Consequences

 The problem becomes especially important when organizations normalize LLM-assisted workflows.

 Initially:

 > human → LLM → human review.

 Over time:

 > human → LLM → standardized workflow.

 Eventually:

 > workflow → LLM → execution.

 At each stage, the organization may increase the amount of responsibility implicitly delegated to the system.

 A capability that was initially “assistance” can therefore become infrastructure.

 The associated risk is not merely technical.

 It becomes organizational.

---

 # 30\. Deskilling and Reliance

 Long-term dependence can also alter human capabilities.

 If personnel repeatedly delegate analysis to AI systems, they may receive less practice independently performing that analysis.

 This can create a feedback relationship:

 $$
\text{greater AI reliance}
\rightarrow
\text{less independent practice}
\rightarrow
\text{greater dependence}
\rightarrow
\text{less verification capacity}.
$$

 The system may therefore become more vulnerable precisely as it becomes more accustomed to AI assistance.

 A recent systematic review of human-in-the-loop AI similarly identifies automation bias and reduced error detection as concerns when users adapt to high AI accuracy.

---

 # 31\. Responsibility and the “Human in the Loop”

 Merely retaining a human in the workflow does not necessarily solve the problem.

 A nominal human reviewer can become a rubber stamp.

 The meaningful question is:

 > **Does the human possess the information, authority, time, expertise, and independence necessary to disagree with the model?**

 If not, the human may function less as an independent decision-maker and more as a procedural endpoint.

 Thus:

 $$
\text{human presence} \neq \text{human independence}.
$$

 Effective human oversight requires genuine capacity for intervention.

---

