 # 33\. A Layered Risk Model

 The framework can be summarized as six layers.

 ## Layer 1: Input skew

 The information supplied to the system is incomplete, misleading, selective, or otherwise distorted.

 ## Layer 2: Interpretive skew

 The model produces a systematically distorted representation or conclusion.

 ## Layer 3: Planning skew

 The distorted representation affects the proposed plan.

 ## Layer 4: Reliance skew

 Humans or software place excessive confidence in the resulting plan.

 ## Layer 5: Execution skew

 The plan produces actions that would not have occurred under a more accurate assessment.

 ## Layer 6: Feedback skew

 The consequences of those actions are interpreted through the same distorted framework.

 The last stage is particularly important because it can stabilize the original error.

