 # 39\. Why More Capable Models Do Not Eliminate the Problem

 Greater reasoning capability can reduce some classes of errors.

 It does not necessarily eliminate systematic skew.

 A highly capable model may reason more effectively from a distorted premise.

 Indeed:

 $$
\text{better reasoning} + \text{bad premise}
$$

 can produce:

 $$
\text{more sophisticated wrong plan}.
$$

 This is one reason that model capability and epistemic reliability must be treated separately.

 Research on erroneous planning similarly demonstrates that powerful LLMs can still generate plans violating task constraints.

