 # 43\. Discussion

 The central contribution of this framework is to shift the focus from **whether an LLM is biased or inaccurate** toward **how model distortion propagates through a decision system**.

 An LLM is rarely the final endpoint of consequential planning.

 It is often an intermediate component.

 The relevant unit of analysis is therefore not necessarily:

 > “Is the model accurate?”

 but:

 > “What happens downstream when this model is wrong, skewed, overtrusted, or systematically influenced?”

 This distinction is essential because model accuracy alone cannot capture dependency.

 A model could be highly accurate on average and still create significant risk if:

 - its failures cluster around consequential situations;
- its outputs are systematically trusted;
- its errors are difficult to detect;
- or its recommendations control downstream action.

 Conversely, a moderately imperfect model could be useful if its outputs are treated as provisional and independently verified.

 Thus:

 $$
\text{system reliability}
\neq
\text{model accuracy alone}.
$$

 Rather:

 $$
\text{system reliability}
=
f(\text{model quality},\text{information quality},\text{reliance},\text{verification},\text{architecture},\text{consequence}).
$$

---

 # 44\. Conclusion

 The central risk associated with skewed large language models is not simply that an AI system might “give a wrong answer.”

 The deeper risk is **causal propagation**.

 A distortion can begin as:

 $$
\text{skewed information}.
$$

 It can become:

 $$
\text{skewed interpretation}.
$$

 The interpretation can become:

 $$
\text{skewed planning}.
$$

 The plan can become:

 $$
\text{human or automated reliance}.
$$

 Reliance can become:

 $$
\text{execution}.
$$

 Execution can alter the environment:

 $$
\text{execution} \rightarrow \text{new evidence}.
$$

 And that evidence can be interpreted by the same system:

 $$
\text{new evidence} \rightarrow \text{reinforced model}.
$$

 The resulting system is capable of turning a relatively small epistemic distortion into a substantial operational divergence.

 This phenomenon is not wholly novel. The literature on automation bias has long demonstrated that humans can over-rely on decision-support systems and accept erroneous recommendations, particularly when tasks are complex or verification is difficult.  PubMed Central (PMC)+1  What is distinctive about LLMs is the combination of broad domain coverage, natural-language interaction, contextual adaptation, persuasive explanation, and increasingly direct integration into planning and execution systems.

 NIST's treatment of generative-AI confabulation illustrates the same fundamental concern from a risk-management perspective: generated information can be false while being presented confidently, and users may act upon it, particularly in consequential contexts.

 The emergence of sycophancy adds another layer. If a model systematically adjusts its responses toward a user's expressed beliefs, then the user can inadvertently—or deliberately—become part of the model's epistemic distortion mechanism. Recent empirical work finds such behavior in multiple contemporary models and in multi-turn interaction.

     The resulting problem can therefore be understood as an **epistemic-control problem**.

 Who controls the information?

 Who frames the question?

 Who determines which alternatives are considered?

 Who verifies the premises?

 Who validates the plan?

 Who authorizes execution?

 Who interprets the results?

 And, critically, does the system contain any genuinely independent mechanism capable of detecting a common error shared across all of those stages?

 The most important safeguard is consequently not simply “keep a human in the loop.”

 It is to maintain **independent opportunities for reality to contradict the model**.

 A robust AI-assisted planning architecture should allow:

 $$
\text{model}
\neq
\text{sole source of evidence},
$$

 $$
\text{model}
\neq
\text{sole evaluator of its own reasoning},
$$

 $$
\text{model}
\neq
\text{sole determinant of execution},
$$

 and:

 $$
\text{model confidence}
\neq
\text{evidence of correctness}.
$$

 The broader theoretical conclusion is that **skew becomes especially consequential when it is coupled to dependency**.

 A model's error is an epistemic event.

 A human's reliance turns that event into a decision.

 A plan turns the decision into an organized sequence of actions.

 Execution turns that sequence into a change in the world.

 And feedback can subsequently make the original distortion harder to recognize.

 The appropriate object of safety analysis is therefore not merely the LLM.

 It is the **entire socio-technical chain through which model outputs become beliefs, plans, decisions, actions, and new evidence**.

 That chain determines whether a model's imperfections remain harmless informational noise or become the initiating condition of a consequential failure.

